```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: ROS 2 (rmw_zenoh default in Jazzy, backported) and standalone zenoh deployments with slow subscribers or multi-hop router topologies — e.g., camera/IMU publisher to perception/control subscriber with processing lag, or multi-robot/WAN via zenoh router where intermediate hop buffers overflow.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real win on contract violation and silent failure mode; microbenchmark on loss magnitude. The split-default (DEFAULT_PUSH=Drop vs DEFAULT_REQUEST=Block, source-verified core/mod.rs:631-657) silently flips reliability between get() (Block) and put() (Drop). CongestionControl::Block measurably fails end-to-end: Block still 50.5% loss (put_ok=N, put_err=0) and 3-node router Block=63.8% loss (put_ok=200000) proves per-hop≠end-to-end. Drops are fully silent: RUST_LOG=zenoh=debug 0 lines during 43.7% loss. The 41-64% loss rate is microbenchmark-specific (8B tcp/127.0.0.1, artificial slow_us=100-500, needs N=200K to overflow router), but the per-hop mechanism and Block non-propagation survive in production router hops. Offload-to-channel→0% loss localizes the trigger to synchronous-callback congestion — a realistic ROS 2 anti-pattern, but mitigable.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Users must (1) offload all subscriber work to channels (never block callback) — contradicts many ROS 2 tutorials/examples; (2) distrust CongestionControl::Block across hops (documented "wait" does not propagate); (3) add application-level drop monitoring (zenoh emits no error/log/NULL). Alternatively, zenoh must change defaults, propagate backpressure, or at least log drops — friction migrating from DDS end-to-end reliability semantics. Router operators must size buffers for sustained load or accept per-hop loss.]
RATIONALE: Packet L0: Block-knob arm slow_us=200 Block=50.5% loss (vs Drop=40.8%) with put_ok=N, put_err=0; 3-node router PUBLISHER-BLOCK=63.8% loss (put_ok=200000) proves Block does not propagate through router hop; source-verified split default (core/mod.rs:631-657); RUST_LOG=debug 0 lines during 43.7% loss (genuinely uninstrumented); offload-to-channel 0% loss (synchronous-callback mechanism, honest weakening). The composition (split-default contract-flip + Block-not-end-to-end + silent) is not "best-effort drops" folklore; it is an undocumented hazard for ROS 2 adopters. Magnitude is microbenchmark-inflated, but the contract violation is operationally meaningful for safety-critical robotics where silent data loss is unacceptable. (Prompt version v001)
```
